66,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option A is the most stable for the fixed costs, but it doesn't address the fuel imbalance we found. Option B is the most mathematically fair, as it ties payment directly to actual usage and distance.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option B is a mathematical dream, but a social nightmare. If we return to the habit of meticulously auditing every kilometer, we risk recreating that atmosphere of accusation I mentioned earlier. It's a recipe for resentment.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is too focused on the math and ignores the state of the car. If we only track distance, there is still no guarantee the car will be clean or fueled for the next person.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,Shion,,"Option B is the only logical choice. We've already established that usage is wildly uneven, especially on weekends. Why should someone who barely drives pay the same as a heavy user? It's the only way to ensure actual fairness.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm worried about Option B too. If we have to record every single distance and calculate it, won't that just mean more time spent untangling the figures every night? It sounds... really exhausting.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Daisuke, we can simplify the recording process to reduce the burden. While Option A is simple, it forces people to pay for days they don't use, which is essentially subsidizing others. Option B ensures no one is unfairly burdened.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, simplicity is often a mask for inefficiency. Option B doesn't just burden us with math; it burdens us with the psychological weight of ""owing"" for every single kilometer. It turns a shared resource into a series of micro-transactions.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, micro-transactions are a small price to pay for reliability. However, Option A is the only one that guarantees I know exactly when the car is available. If I have my own days, the unpredictability of the midweek morning disappears.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, you're prioritizing your own convenience over financial logic. Option A is just a different form of guesswork; it doesn't solve the fuel imbalance, it just hides it behind a schedule. We need a system based on reality, not a calendar.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Option A might be better than B because it's simpler. If we have our own days, maybe we won't have to spend so much time every evening arguing about who owes what. It feels... less stressful.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Minako,,"Option A is simple, but it doesn't solve the core issue of who is subsidizing whom. If we want a truly even split that accounts for the 280,000 yen and the fuel gap, Option B is the only objective path.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Minako, objectivity is a cold comfort when it leads to a house full of accountants instead of friends. Option C, while perhaps less ""fair"" on paper, removes the friction of auditing and the guilt of the ""favor"" economy by centralizing responsibility.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, Option C is too risky. Centralizing responsibility just means one person bears the burden of the car's condition, and the rest of us are back to hoping they've kept it ready. I prefer Option A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Benjamin, Option C is just a return to the same guesswork we have now, only with one person acting as the gatekeeper. It's illogical. Option B is the only way to stop the subsidizing and get an honest picture of the costs.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I don't think I like Option C. It sounds like it would just make that one person really stressed, and I'd feel bad asking them every time. I think Option A is the safest... it just feels quieter.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Minako,,"While Option A is quieter, it doesn't resolve the financial imbalance. However, since we must choose a sustainable path, I will prioritize objectivity. I vote for Option B to ensure a truly even split.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I must disagree with the pursuit of absolute objectivity. If we prioritize the ledger over the living room, we will simply trade financial friction for social frost. I vote for Option A; it is the most peaceful compromise.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I cannot support Option B or C. B is a bureaucratic mess that ignores the car's condition, and C creates a bottleneck of dependency. Option A provides the predictability I need for my mornings. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,Shion,,It's disappointing that you're all so afraid of a little math. Option A is just a lazy way to ignore the fact that some of us are being cheated. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
85,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I can't handle the thought of more auditing and long discussions every night. Option B sounds too stressful, and C feels unfair to the keeper. I'll vote for Option A. It's the simplest way to keep the peace.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
